@node	0.2, 0.3, 0.1, Chapter 0
@section Values, names, and functions

This section covers literals, bindings, functions, closures, higher-order
functions, booleans, comparison operators, conditionals, operator
precedence, and the number model this book uses. Everything here reappears
in @ref{1.1} and nothing else from TypeScript is assumed.

@heading Literals and names

The book's numbers are @code{number}, its truth values are @code{boolean},
and its text is a @code{string}. Underscores may group digits in any
numeric literal, and a template literal splices a value into text:

@example typescript
486
6.626_070_15e-34
true
`n is $@{n@}`
@end example

@noindent
A name is bound with @code{const}, which cannot be reassigned; the
reassignable @code{let} exists and has a section of its own (@ref{0.5}). A
@newterm{binding} is not a cell the program later assigns into: it names a
value, once, for the whole scope that contains it.

@example typescript
const size = 2;

5 + 3 + size
// => 10
@end example

@heading Functions

A function is an arrow from its parameters to its body. An expression body
writes the computation after the arrow; a block body is a braced sequence
and returns explicitly:

@example typescript
const square = (x: number): number => x * x;

const hypotenuseSquared = (a: number, b: number): number => @{
  const a2 = square(a);
  const b2 = square(b);
  return a2 + b2;
@};

square(21)
// => 441

square(2 + 5)
// => 49

square(square(3))
// => 81
@end example

@noindent
A function may be declared inside another, where it sees the local names
of its enclosing scope, and a local @code{const} introduces a name visible
only inside its own block. One restriction matters from chapter 1 on: a
@code{const} arrow is not usable above its own declaration, so two
mutually recursive local functions must both be bound before the first
call; @ref{0.3} returns to the shapes that survive.

A function value closes over the names around it, so @code{makeAdder}
builds a family of functions from one declaration:

@example typescript
const makeAdder = (n: number): ((x: number) => number) => (x) => x + n;

const add5 = makeAdder(5);
const add12 = makeAdder(12);

add5(1)
// => 6

add12(1)
// => 13
@end example

@noindent
The type @code{(x: number) => number} is a function type: a procedure is
an ordinary value. A @code{type} alias names a shape that recurs, a
function can take functions as arguments, and a function can return one:

@example typescript
type Mapper = (x: number) => number;

const sumOver = (f: Mapper, a: number, b: number): number => @{
  let total = 0;
  for (let k = a; k <= b; k += 1) @{
    total += f(k);
  @}
  return total;
@};

const twice = (m: Mapper): Mapper => (x) => m(m(x));

sumOver(square, 1, 3)
// => 14

twice(square)(2)
// => 16
@end example

@noindent
Higher-order programs from @ref{1.3} translate onto this directly, and the
composed procedures of @ref{3.1} are closures of exactly the
@code{makeAdder} shape.

@heading Booleans, comparisons, and conditionals

The comparison operators are @code{=== !== < > <= >=}, the connectives are
@code{&& || !}, and @code{===} compares the primitives by value; for the
objects of @ref{0.4} it compares by identity. The conditional expression
@code{? :} produces a value, so the book's @code{cond} and @code{if}
translate onto it in line; an @code{if} statement carries a branch's work
and returns explicitly:

@example typescript
const sign = (n: number): string =>
  n > 0 ? "positive" : n < 0 ? "negative" : "zero";

const classify = (n: number): string => @{
  if (n === 0) @{
    return "zero";
  @}
  if (n >= 1 && n <= 9) @{
    return "small";
  @}
  return "large";
@};

sign(-3)
// => "negative"

classify(7)
// => "small"

classify(42)
// => "large"
@end example

@noindent
Operator precedence follows the customary infix reading: @code{* / %}
bind tighter than @code{+ -}, comparison binds looser than arithmetic,
and @code{&&} binds tighter than @code{||}. Parentheses win, and the
book's listings use them wherever two readings are plausible.

@heading The number model

A number is a 64-bit IEEE 754 float. Small integers are exact: every
integer from @code{-(2 ** 53 - 1)} through @code{2 ** 53 - 1} is
represented exactly, and @code{Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER} names the top of
that range. Past it, integers silently round, and a program cannot tell:

@example typescript
2 ** 53 + 1 === 2 ** 53
// => true
@end example

@noindent
The division rule is the one the book's first sessions need: @code{/} is
always real division, and integer division is a choice the program states:

@example typescript
const halfOf = (n: number): number => Math.trunc(n / 2);
const realHalf = (n: number): number => n / 2;

halfOf(9)
// => 4

realHalf(9)
// => 4.5
@end example

@footnote{Floats round: @code{0.1 + 0.2} is @code{0.30000000000000004},
not @code{0.3}. The rounding is invisible to a program that only prints
integers, which is what makes it worth a rule rather than a warning.}

Exact integers past the bound are @code{bigint} values, written with an
@code{n} suffix, and @code{bigint} keeps arithmetic exact by refusing to
mix with @code{number} without an explicit conversion:

@example typescript
const factorialBig = (n: bigint): bigint =>
  n <= 1n ? 1n : n * factorialBig(n - 1n);

factorialBig(18n)
// => 6402373705728000n

factorialBig(25n)
// => 15511210043330985984000000n
@end example

@noindent
Small counters and indices stay @code{number}; everything the main text
prints as an exact integer past @code{2 ** 53} is a @code{bigint}. The
runnable version of this section's listings is
@file{packages/ch0/src/02-values-functions.ts}, asserted by the test file
beside it.

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.1}Exercise 0.1:} The three interaction sessions
below are ordinary expressions of this chapter's language. Before running
anything, predict the response of each expression, reading it as a tree of
operations the way this section introduced; then evaluate the sessions in
order and collect the responses. Implement @code{ex_0_01} so that it
returns the numeric responses in the order they occur. The second
session's comparison answers @code{false}, which is not a number, so that
response is not collected; @ref{0.8}'s worked transcript shows it.

@example typescript
486
100
5 + 3 + 4
10 - 9
2 * 4 + (4 - 6)
@end example

@example typescript
const a = 3;

const b = a + 1;

a + b + a * b
a === b
b > a && b < a * b ? b : a
a === 4 ? 6 : b === 4 ? 6 + 7 + a : 25 + a
2 + (b > a ? b : a)
(a > b ? a : b > a ? b : -1) * (a + 1)
@end example

@example typescript
const square = (x: number): number => x * x;

square(21)
square(2 + 5)
square(square(3))
@end example

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.2}Exercise 0.2:} Implement @code{compose} and
@code{repeated} over one-argument procedures on numbers:
@code{compose(f, g)(x)} applies @code{g} and then @code{f}, and
@code{repeated(f, n)(x)} applies @code{f} exactly @code{n} times, with
@code{repeated(f, 0)(x)} the identity. Prove them with fast-check
property tests: state what must hold for generated inputs, and check the
@code{n = 0} boundary and both orders of @code{compose} against
hand-computed cases.

